gbhv(){return"On the beat, or off?"},
gbhu(){return"Usually the notes land right ON the beats \u2014 count 1-2-3-4 and each note lands on a number. Steady and square."},
gbht(){return'Syncopation pushes notes OFF the beat, onto the "and" in between. The accent lands where your ear didnt expect it \u2014 thats the kick you feel in pop and jazz.'},
gbhR(){return"Two or three in a beat"},
gbhP(){return'Normally a beat splits into TWO even halves: "1-and". Two eighth notes.'},
gbhQ(){return'A triplet squeezes THREE equal notes into that same beat: "trip-o-let". It gets a little 3 above it.'},
gbgM(){return"Decorating a note"},
gbgN(){return"Ornaments are little signs that dress up a note. A trill (tr) shakes quickly between the note and the one just above it."},
gbgO(){return"A turn (a sideways S) curls AROUND the note: the note above, the note, the note below, then back. A mordent is just one quick flick up and back."},
ga58(){return"Label the Form"},
gasJ(){return"Hear the sections; name the shape (ABA\u2026)"},
gb2M(){return"What is the form? (same colour = same tune)"},
gb2L(){return"Listen"},
gbfQ(){return"The shape of a piece"},
gbfP(){return"Music is built from sections. Here is a little tune \u2014 call it section A. Whenever it comes back, it is A again."},
gbfO(){return"A different tune is a new letter \u2014 section B. Tune, different tune, then the first tune again makes the form A-B-A. Lots of songs are shaped this way!"},
ga5A(){return"Which Mode?"},
gata(){return"Major, minor or Dorian?"},
gbbm(){return"Listen! Which mode is it?"},
ga2c(){return"Major"},
ga2d(){return"Minor"},
gbbl(){return"Dorian"},
gbgI(){return"Three colours of scale"},
gbgG(){return"A major scale sounds bright and happy. Listen to it climb."},
gbgH(){return"A minor scale sounds darker \u2014 its 3rd, 6th and 7th steps sit a little lower."},
gbgF(){return"Dorian is like minor, but its 6th step is raised \u2014 so it sounds minor with a brighter twist. That one note is the whole secret!"},
